  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis identifies **Arachidonic Acid Metabolism (KEGG map00590)** as the dominant affected pathway, with all six polyunsaturated fatty-acid derivatives (15-Deoxy-d-12,14-PGJ2, 15-HETE, 5-HETE, 11,12,15-THETA, Leukotriene A4, and the parent arachidonate) achieving a highly significant hit (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment ≈ 735). CDP (C00112) is the sole outlier, connecting instead to pyrimidine metabolism, and is excluded from the core lipid story.\n\n**Key driver compounds, verified by pathway membership:**\n\n- **Arachidonic acid (C00219)** is the obligate entry point and confirmed member of KEGG Arachidonic Acid Metabolism.\n- **Leukotriene A4 (C00909)** and **15-HETE (C04742)** are both confirmed members of the same pathway.\n- **15-Deoxy-d-12,14-PGJ2 (C14717)** is confirmed in Arachidonic Acid Metabolism; notably it has zero downstream neighbours in the reaction graph, consistent with its role as a terminal cyclopentenone prostaglandin that does not feed forward into further reactions.\n\n**Verified upstream/downstream relationships via KEGG reaction graph:**\n\n- Arachidonic acid → Leukotriene A4: reachable in 2 hops (via C05356), direction bidirectional, confirming LTA4 is a downstream metabolite of arachidonate.\n- Arachidonic acid → 15-HETE: reachable in 2 hops (via C05966), forward direction, confirming 15-HETE as a downstream monooxygenation product.\n- Arachidonic acid → 15-Deoxy-d-12,14-PGJ2: not reachable in the reaction graph (no path within 6 hops), consistent with PGJ2 derivation from PGD2 via separate dehydratase steps not captured in the compact graph.\n\n**Biological significance:** The co-enrichment of 5-HETE and Leukotriene A4 implicates the **5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) arm** of arachidonate metabolism, a major pro-inflammatory leukotriene biosynthetic route. The simultaneous elevation of cyclopentenone prostaglandins (15d-PGJ2) and multiple HETE positional isomers (5-, 12-, 15-HETEs) suggests the treatment perturbs not only the 5-LOX pathway but also the **cytochrome-P450 epoxygenase/hydroperoxylase** and **cyclooxygenase** branches, indicating a broad lipid-mediator shift consistent with active inflammation and/or the initiation of resolution programmes. CDP elevation may reflect downstream nucleotide signalling secondary to cellular activation.",
